{"pageNumber":"1","pageRowStart":"0","pageSize":"3","recordCount":8546,"records":[{"id":70282754,"text":"70282754 - 2027 - Ground failure resulting from earthquake ground motions","interactions":[],"lastModifiedDate":"2026-09-30T13:17:19.833183","indexId":"70282754","displayToPublicDate":"2026-08-08T15:43:02","publicationYear":"2027","noYear":false,"publicationType":{"id":5,"text":"Book chapter"},"publicationSubtype":{"id":24,"text":"Book Chapter"},"chapter":"4","title":"Ground failure resulting from earthquake ground motions","docAbstract":"<p>The ground failure resulting from an earthquake, at all scales, is ultimately controlled by the details of near-surface earthquake ground motions, the spatially distributed mechanical characteristics of surface materials and how the two interact. This chapter introduces the aspects of ground-motion seismology that are most relevant to the triggering of different types of ground failure, such as landslides and liquefaction. In particular, the chapter focuses on different sources of local modification (e.g. amplification) of earthquake ground motions in the shallow subsurface, known as site effects. The chapter ends with an overview of the controls on, general spatial patterns of, and approaches to regional modelling of the manifestation of ground failure that may be triggered by an earthquake.</p>","largerWorkType":{"id":4,"text":"Book"},"largerWorkTitle":"Geomorphic impacts of earthquakes: Post-seismic landscape evolution","largerWorkSubtype":{"id":15,"text":"Monograph"},"language":"English","publisher":"Elsevier","doi":"10.1016/B978-0-443-40375-0.00008-6","isbn":"9780443403750","usgsCitation":"Allstadt, K.E., and Thompson, E.M., 2027, Ground failure resulting from earthquake ground motions, chap. 4 <i>of</i> Geomorphic impacts of earthquakes: Post-seismic landscape evolution, p. 41-79, https://doi.org/10.1016/B978-0-443-40375-0.00008-6.","productDescription":"39 p.","startPage":"41","endPage":"79","ipdsId":"IP-186048","costCenters":[{"id":78941,"text":"Geologic Hazards Science Center - Landslides / Earthquake Geology","active":true,"usgs":true}],"links":[{"id":512569,"rank":1,"type":{"id":24,"text":"Thumbnail"},"url":"https://pubs.usgs.gov/thumbnails/outside_thumb.jpg"}],"noUsgsAuthors":false,"publicationStatus":"PW","contributors":{"authors":[{"text":"Allstadt, Kate E. 0000-0003-4977-5248","orcid":"https://orcid.org/0000-0003-4977-5248","contributorId":138704,"corporation":false,"usgs":true,"family":"Allstadt","given":"Kate","email":"","middleInitial":"E.","affiliations":[{"id":300,"text":"Geologic Hazards Science Center","active":true,"usgs":true}],"preferred":true,"id":973457,"contributorType":{"id":1,"text":"Authors"},"rank":1},{"text":"Thompson, Eric M. 0000-0002-6943-4806 emthompson@usgs.gov","orcid":"https://orcid.org/0000-0002-6943-4806","contributorId":150897,"corporation":false,"usgs":true,"family":"Thompson","given":"Eric","email":"emthompson@usgs.gov","middleInitial":"M.","affiliations":[{"id":300,"text":"Geologic Hazards Science Center","active":true,"usgs":true}],"preferred":true,"id":973458,"contributorType":{"id":1,"text":"Authors"},"rank":2}]}}
,{"id":70283916,"text":"70283916 - 2026 - Environmental drivers of deforming bedrock landslides in southern Alaska","interactions":[],"lastModifiedDate":"2026-11-23T15:19:15.743337","indexId":"70283916","displayToPublicDate":"2026-11-20T09:13:39","publicationYear":"2026","noYear":false,"publicationType":{"id":2,"text":"Article"},"publicationSubtype":{"id":10,"text":"Journal Article"},"seriesTitle":{"id":17801,"text":"npj Natural Hazards","active":true,"publicationSubtype":{"id":10}},"title":"Environmental drivers of deforming bedrock landslides in southern Alaska","docAbstract":"<p><span>Large moving bedrock subaerial landslides pose considerable hazards to people, infrastructure, and environments if they fail rapidly and run out over land or into water bodies and produce damaging waves. Identification of these features across the landscape can determine physiographic and environmental factors that influence landslide occurrence, aiding landslide hazard and risk assessments. To accomplish this, we apply persistent scatterer interferometric synthetic aperture radar (PSInSAR) methods with three years of Sentinel−1 data (2019 − 2021) to map 239 deforming bedrock landslides across 617,000 km</span><sup>2</sup><span> of southern Alaska and describe their physiographic setting. Identified landslides range in volume from approximately 0.4–1860 million cubic meters. The landslides are predominantly in sedimentary rock located on warmer south-facing slopes, and may be particularly sensitive to changes in hydrologic, thermal, and permafrost conditions that affect slope stability. Frequency-ratio analysis demonstrates that the identified landslides have a positive association with transient permafrost conditions and are 3.6 times more likely to occur within 2 km of a glacier, emphasizing the influence of ongoing cryosphere degradation on rock slope stability. Climate projections indicate substantial reductions in near-surface permafrost and glaciers over the next half century, which will modify landslide susceptibility throughout southern Alaska. The new regional inventory of hundreds of recently deforming bedrock landslides can help to refine susceptibility assessments and prioritize future analyses and risk mitigation efforts in Alaska.</span></p>","language":"English","publisher":"Nature","doi":"10.1038/s44304-026-00296-3","usgsCitation":"Schaefer, L.N., Staley, D.M., Kim, J., Barnhart, K.R., and Lu, Z., 2026, Environmental drivers of deforming bedrock landslides in southern Alaska: npj Natural Hazards, v. 3, 90, 15 p., https://doi.org/10.1038/s44304-026-00296-3.","productDescription":"90, 15 p.","ipdsId":"IP-191280","costCenters":[{"id":78941,"text":"Geologic Hazards Science Center - Landslides / Earthquake Geology","active":true,"usgs":true}],"links":[{"id":518577,"type":{"id":24,"text":"Thumbnail"},"url":"https://pubs.usgs.gov/thumbnails/outside_thumb.jpg"}],"country":"United States","state":"Alaska","geographicExtents":"{
