,{"id":70283907,"text":"70283907 - 2026 - Reproducible event-bed correlation with uncertainty: pyCoreRelator","interactions":[],"lastModifiedDate":"2026-11-19T14:52:19.048724","indexId":"70283907","displayToPublicDate":"2026-11-17T07:42:58","publicationYear":"2026","noYear":false,"publicationType":{"id":2,"text":"Article"},"publicationSubtype":{"id":10,"text":"Journal Article"},"seriesTitle":{"id":3369,"text":"Sedimentology","active":true,"publicationSubtype":{"id":10}},"displayTitle":"Reproducible event-bed correlation with uncertainty: <i>pyCoreRelator</i>","title":"Reproducible event-bed correlation with uncertainty: pyCoreRelator","docAbstract":"<p><span>Stratigraphic correlation is fundamental to Earth science, yet conventional approaches rely heavily on subjective visual data matching. Automated algorithms such as dynamic time warping (DTW) provide reproducibility, but when sequences are subdivided into clearly defined stratigraphic units, DTW alone struggles to explore a vast range of correlation possibilities among these units and evaluate the optimal alignment. To address this, we introduce </span><i>pyCoreRelator</i><span>, an open-source Python package designed for quantitative and reproducible pairwise stratigraphic correlation. The package can handle core imagery and geophysical log data, and systematically explores all feasible inter-unit correlations, honouring geochronological constraints and accounting for possible lateral discontinuities of the beds. Correlation scenarios are ranked using quantitative similarity metrics, including normalised DTW cost and Pearson's </span><i>r</i><span>, to identify the optimal solution that minimises the degree of variation in sedimentation rates and erosion while maximising similarity. Furthermore, </span><i>pyCoreRelator</i><span> statistically evaluates the geological significance of these correlations by comparing them to the expected background lithological similarity in the natural environment, represented by stochastic baselines generated through Markov Chain Monte Carlo stratigraphic simulations. We demonstrate this workflow for an event-bed correlation case study with radiocarbon-dated marine turbidites used previously to derive a chronology of large earthquakes in the Cascadia Subduction Zone. Pairwise analysis of closely spaced core sites yielded robust, statistically significant correlations that align with prior human interpretations. However, within the same channel system, the quality and certainty of correlations degrade as the distance between core sites increases and, in some cases, worsen with the inclusion of age constraints. These results indicate that current data cannot unambiguously confirm margin-wide synchronous turbidite deposition, urging caution when relying on visual, long-distance correlations to define regional earthquake histories. These results underscore the efficiency and effectiveness of </span><i>pyCoreRelator</i><span> in identifying meaningful stratigraphic correlations through a transparent, probabilistic approach that can be applied across diverse geoscience fields.</span></p>","language":"English","publisher":"Wiley","doi":"10.1111/sed.70163","usgsCitation":"Lai, L.S., Sylvester, Z., Covault, J.A., Gomberg, J.S., and Nieminski, N.M., 2026, Reproducible event-bed correlation with uncertainty: pyCoreRelator: Sedimentology, 21 p., https://doi.org/10.1111/sed.70163.","productDescription":"21 p.","ipdsId":"IP-191949","costCenters":[{"id":237,"text":"Earthquake Science Center","active":true,"usgs":true}],"links":[{"id":517821,"rank":0,"type":{"id":40,"text":"Open Access Publisher Index Page"},"url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/sed.70163","text":"Publisher Index Page"},{"id":517603,"type":{"id":24,"text":"Thumbnail"},"url":"https://pubs.usgs.gov/thumbnails/outside_thumb.jpg"}],"country":"United States","state":"Oregon, Washington","otherGeospatial":"Cascadia Subduction Zone","geographicExtents":"{
